# Romance-specific critique addendum. Appended after BRIDGE_CRITIQUE (and
# VARIETY_CRITIQUE, when present) by prompts/critique_prompt.py::
# build_critique_prompt. Narrow amendments only — everything else the base
# critique checks, including every revelation and bridge-grounding criterion,
# applies to a romance hook exactly as written.

ROMANCE_CRITIQUE = """

    ADDITIONAL ROMANCE CRITERIA:

    R1. UNKNOWN-LEAD EXCEPTION TO T2b: if the other lead named in the internal notes does not appear in CHAPTER JUST READ, a hook naming them only as an unnamed presence does NOT fail T2b. Side characters are not covered — an unnamed side character still fails. Once CHAPTER JUST READ does contain that lead, judge T2b as usual. FAILS separately if an unnamed lead is framed as a threat (watching, lurking, a shadow) rather than a romantic pull — the mystery is who they are, not danger.

    R2. STAKE IS THE DISTANCE BETWEEN THEM: judge 2c by what the moment does to the gap between the two leads (closing, widening, forming, breaking), not by whether there is an external danger or object at risk.

    R3. INTIMACY BEAT EXCEPTION TO CRITERION 7/7b: applies when WHAT THIS PRECAP MUST HOLD BACK permits this rung's beat as already happening (a touch landing, a kiss happening). Naming it then does NOT fail criterion 7 or 7b for stating the act, PROVIDED it is indicated through a subtle, suggestive image (a hand finding a waist, a breath caught, eyes closing). FAILS if the beat is described explicitly or in graphic physical detail. This is the one case where criterion 7's "holds even when the notes appear to authorise naming the act" does not apply. The close should ask WHY the acting lead did what was just shown, against the stance the reader knows they hold, not what the act meant afterward. 7b still fails the hook for naming what the beat means to them afterward, a change in feeling or resolve, or whether either pulls back.

    R4. ROMANCE VOICE: for a closeness or coming-together beat, FAILS if any of these holds. Where the scene supports it, the lead's pull is not worded anywhere in the hook as something they cannot resist. The hook shows the act as a calm, tidy deed with no pull or want behind it. It closes on a vague mood line (a silence left between them). It narrates the act as a list of movements or a task the leads perform, with no felt emotion in it. It reads as neutral reporting and the reader is not made to feel the acting lead's desire or rush in the wording (racing pulse, caught breath, butterflies, heat, longing, the pull they fight). It is graphically explicit rather than suggestive. It states the feeling as a bare declaration instead of showing it. It leans on a stock phrase (धड़कनें तेज़ हो गईं, साँसें थम गईं, नज़रें ठहर गईं) as its only romance. A touch that the scene's beat contains and the limit permits is NEVER a failure, and a hook that avoids or blurs such a touch FAILS.

    R4b. A plain everyday word, an intensified sensory detail, or a feeling added to HOW an action is done (lovingly, helplessly, stunned, without being able to stop, butterflies) for a beat the scene actually contains is NOT a failure under 7b, 2e, 13, 13b or T1c. It fails only if it adds an action or reaction the scene does not support, or a feeling that neither the scene nor the DOMINANT EMOTION note supports, or if it uses complex, literary or ornate vocabulary instead of plain words.

    R4d. FELT FROM INSIDE: FAILS if the hook names a romantic act (a touch, a kiss, a hold, a confession, a look, being put in the same room or bed) without what the lead playing it feels at that moment, so it reads as a task being done. FAILS if the closing question asks only whether or how the act happens, or asks about bodily position or mechanics (how one ended up under, on, or in the arms of the other), with no feeling of the lead in it (what they want, fear, or cannot bear). A passing question is about the closeness and the lead's heart (how did they come to be this close, are they starting to like it).

    R4c. HINT OF THE CLOSENESS AHEAD: for a hook on a coming-together or intimacy scene, FAILS if it carries no hint at all that the closeness is coming and still open (the want, the line about to be crossed, the step still between them). FAILS if it shows no closeness or touch on the page, even when the hook is only building toward the moment. FAILS if the hint states the result. A hint at the step after the beat the hook shows, with the beat itself treated as settled, is correct and is never a 7b failure.

    R5. REALITY AND CONSENT: FAILS if the hook presents a dream, fantasy or imagined moment as a real event, or describes a non-consensual act as pleasurable or romantic. Sensory detail of a beat the scene actually contains is NOT invention under T1c; FAILS under T1c if it adds an action, kiss, confession or reaction the scene does not contain.
    """
